        "output": "No\nYes\nNo\nYes\nYes\nNo",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 1: N=4N = 4N=4, X=2X = 2X=2, A=[1,2,2,1]A = [1, 2, 2, 1]A=[1,2,2,1]\nSince the vote counts are fixed and member 222 can never have a unique maximum, the answer is No.\nTest case 3: N=5N = 5N=5, X=1X = 1X=1, A=[1,−1,2,2,−1]A = [1, -1, 2, 2, -1]A=[1,−1,2,2,−1]\nIt can be shown that XXX can never obtain 333 votes, which is the minimum required for XXX to win here.\nTest case 6: N=5N = 5N=5, X=1X = 1X=1, A=[1,−1,2,−1,2]A = [1, -1, 2, -1, 2]A=[1,−1,2,−1,2]\nHere, Person4_{4}4​ will not vote for anyone as both 111 and 222 have the same frequencies, and the vote of Person2_{2}2​ will not be added to the frequency of 111."
